{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_aw7hqfyuvldzddf654wzheni4prjzvtlhi2eirp3zznaoxbfvg4a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_016trGuDjfFSYABGqMKh1osY","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-M3fAKX/translation/ERRORS.md","content":"# ERRORS.md — Phase C error / rejection surface table\n\nDerived mechanically from `c_src/src/lib.c`. Grep results for the usual\nerror-surface constructs:\n\n```\n$ grep -cE 'assert|NULL|nullptr|if *\\(' c_src/src/lib.c   -> 0\n$ grep -nE 'default:'               c_src/src/lib.c       -> 98, 222, 269\n$ grep -nE '#if|#ifdef'             c_src/src/lib.c       -> (none)\n```\n\nThis library has **no** error enum, **no** `RETURN_ERROR` macro, **no** `return -1`\n/ `return NULL` failure path, **no** `assert`, **no** null check, and **no**\nexplicit range check or min/max constant. It contains zero `if` statements.\n\nIts rejection behaviour is therefore expressed entirely through the three\n`switch` `default:` arms and through the unconditional `& 7` index mask. Each row\nbelow is one distinct such rejection, and each is a *silent* rejection — the C\nnever signals failure, it substitutes a fallback value. That fallback is the\ncontract the Rust must reproduce exactly.\n\n| # | function | trigger (the exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result | test | ✅ |\n|---|----------|---------------------------------------------|-------------------|------|----|\n| 1 | `get_predict_func` (`default:` @ line 269) | `pfcn` outside `0..=11` — the `switch` falls to `default:` which does not assign, leaving the `result = 0` initialiser | returns `0` (never `-1`, never an error code) | `err_row1_get_predict_func_out_of_range` | ✅ |\n| 2 | `get_predict_func` (`default:` @ 269) | `pfcn` = `12,13,14,15` — i.e. values that ARE valid for `BTAC1C2_PredictSample`'s FIR arms but are NOT valid for the specialised dispatch | returns `0` | `err_row2_get_predict_func_fir_range_rejected` | ✅ |\n| 3 | `get_predict_func` (`default:` @ 269) | `pfcn` negative (`-1`, `-12`, `INT_MIN`) | returns `0` | `err_row3_get_predict_func_negative` | ✅ |\n| 4 | `get_predict_func` (`default:` @ 269) | `pfcn` = `INT_MAX`, `INT_MIN`, and one step past each end of the valid range (`-1`, `12`) | returns `0` | `err_row4_get_predict_func_extremes_and_off_by_one` | ✅ |\n| 5 | `BTAC1C2_GetPredictFunc` (`default:` @ 222) | `pfcn` outside `0..=11` | returns the *generic* `BTAC1C2_PredictSample` pointer, NOT `NULL` — so a caller can never detect the invalid input by a null test | `err_row5_getpredictfunc_default_returns_generic` | ✅ |\n| 6 | `BTAC1C2_PredictSample` (`default:` @ 98) | `pfcn` outside `0..=15` (e.g. `16`, `-1`, `INT_MIN`, `INT_MAX`) | `pred = 0`, returns `0` — the sample data is ignored entirely | `err_row6_predictsample_default_returns_zero` | ✅ |\n| 7 | `BTAC1C2_PredictSample` FIR arms (`case 12..15` @ 84–97) | `pfcn` in `12..=15` indexes `ridx->firfx[pfcn - 12]`; there is no bounds check, and `pfcn = 16` would read out of the 4-row array — but the `default:` arm catches it first, so `firfx` is never indexed out of range | out-of-range `pfcn` never reaches the FIR arm; `12..=15` map to rows `0..=3` | `err_row7_fir_row_selection_in_bounds` | ✅ |\n| 8 | all 15 predictors — index mask (`& 7`, 96 occurrences) | `idx` arbitrary, incl. negative and `INT_MIN`/`INT_MAX`: `psamp[(i - k) & 7]` is never bounds-checked but the `& 7` mask *unconditionally* confines the subscript to `0..=7`, so no out-of-range read is possible for ANY `idx` | index always in `0..=7`; no rejection, no crash; result is the wrapped element | `err_row8_index_mask_confines_all_idx` | ✅ |\n| 9 | all 15 predictors — `int` overflow | sample values near `INT_MIN`/`INT_MAX` multiplied by coefficients up to 76 overflow `int` (UB in C; gcc wraps two's-complement at `-O0`) | wraps two's-complement; Rust must use `wrapping_*` to match | `err_row9_overflow_wraps_identically` | ✅ |\n| 10 | `BTAC1C2_PredictSample` arms 7/8/9 and 12–15, `BTAC1C2_PredictSample_Pfn7/8/9` | division by the constants `16`, `64`, `256` with a negative numerator — C `/` truncates toward zero, which differs from the arithmetic `>>` used by the other arms | truncation toward zero (NOT floor); divisor is a non-zero constant so division-by-zero is impossible | `err_row10_negative_division_truncates_toward_zero` | ✅ |\n| 11 | `get_predict_func` — out-of-range enum-style value across FFI | `pfcn` is a plain `int`, so any 32-bit value is accepted at the FFI boundary even though only `0..=11` are meaningful; C performs no validation | returns `0` for every value with no valid variant; must not trap or panic | `err_row11_out_of_range_enum_values_across_ffi` | ✅ |\n| 12 | public ABI — null pointers | `get_predict_func(int)` takes **no** pointer parameters, so the classic null-pointer rejection cannot be triggered through the public ABI at all. The internal predictors do take `int *psamp` / `btac1c_idxstate *ridx` and deref them with **no** null check, so passing null is UB in C (segfault, not a rejection) and is deliberately not exercised. `ridx` is only dereferenced by `pfcn` `12..=15`; every other arm ignores it, so a null `ridx` is harmless there — that IS testable and is tested. | no null check exists; null `ridx` tolerated for `pfcn` ∉ `12..=15`, identically in both | `err_row12_null_ridx_tolerated_when_unused` | ✅ |\n\nAll 12 rows have a passing differential test in `tests/phase_c_errors.rs`.\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1394,"cache_read_input_tokens":41829,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1394,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9cc3d074-7e08-4b72-9c53-7efe80847659","uuid":"9938f5f8-dcf0-4c07-8882-1f10f58e8717","timestamp":"2026-08-27T09:0